<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gustav von Overbeck</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1830">1830</a>–<a href="/years/1894">1894</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Baron von Overbeck, Gustav Overbeck</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gustav von Overbeck was a German-born businessman and Austrian consul in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> who, with the British merchant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-dent">Alfred Dent</a>, obtained grants of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a> from the sultans of Brunei and Sulu in 1877 and 1878. Unable to interest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> or other powers in the territory, he sold his share to Dent in 1880, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-north-borneo-company">British North Borneo Company</a> received a royal charter in 1881. He held an Austrian barony.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The 1878 agreement under which Sultan Jamalul Alam granted North Borneo to <b>Baron Gustav von Overbeck</b> and Alfred Dent underlies the Philippine claim to Sabah, formally raised in 1962, and the claims of the sultan's heirs, which remain a source of friction between the Philippines and Malaysia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sulu-sultanate">Sulu Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1877 and 1878 the Austrian consul <b>Baron von Overbeck</b> and the British merchant Alfred Dent obtained grants of northern Borneo from the Brunei Sultanate and the Sulu Sultanate.</blockquote><a href="/state/north-borneo">North Borneo</a></li></ul></section>
